    fullDescription: "Old Phuket Town is the cultural heart of the island and the antidote to the beach resorts, a compact and wonderfully photogenic historic quarter built on the fortunes of the nineteenth-century tin-mining boom. When Chinese immigrant traders and European tin merchants grew rich, they raised elegant Sino-Portuguese shophouses — narrow, deep buildings with ornate stuccoed façades, shuttered upper windows, arched colonnades and pastel paintwork in mint, ochre and dusty pink — and streets such as Thalang, Dibuk, Krabi and Yaowarat still preserve this architecture almost intact. Today the old town has been lovingly revived: the restored shophouses hold hip cafés, cocktail bars, boutique guesthouses, art galleries, tailors and museums, while bright, witty street-art murals hide down the lanes waiting to be discovered. Don't miss Soi Romanee, the prettiest street of all, and the Thai Hua Museum, which tells the story of the town's Chinese heritage inside a grand former school. The area is at its liveliest during the Sunday-evening Walking Street (Lard Yai), when Thalang Road closes to traffic and fills with food stalls, crafts and performers. Wander in the cooler morning to admire the architecture and browse the galleries, pause for a Peranakan coffee, and soak up a side of Phuket — Baba culture, local food and genuine history — that most sun-seekers miss entirely. It is free, endlessly walkable and one of the island's real highlights.",
