    fullDescription: "The Similan Islands are a protected marine national park of eleven granite-and-jungle islands scattered in the Andaman Sea north-west of Phuket, and they are widely regarded as home to the most beautiful beaches and the best diving and snorkelling in all of Thailand. Unlike the karst scenery of Phang Nga and Phi Phi, the Similans are defined by their huge rounded granite boulders, dazzling powder-white sand and water of extraordinary clarity — a pale, luminous turquoise over the shallow reefs deepening to sapphire. Above the water, viewpoints like Sail Rock on Island 8 reward a short scramble with a classic Thailand-postcard panorama; below it lies a world-class underwater realm of vibrant hard and soft corals, swirling schools of fish, turtles, reef sharks and rays that draws divers from across the globe, with dedicated liveaboard trips as well as day excursions. Because the park is strictly protected and vulnerable to monsoon seas, it is open only for the dry half of the year, roughly mid-October to mid-May, and closed entirely in the low season, so plan accordingly. A day trip involves an early start and a road transfer to a pier north of Phuket followed by a fast speedboat ride, making for a long day, but for anyone who prioritises pristine nature, snorkelling and diving over nightlife and crowds, the reward is well worth it. National-park fees apply on top of the tour price.",
