    fullDescription: "Phang Nga Bay is one of the great natural wonders of Southeast Asia and, for many, the single most memorable day trip from Phuket. Sheltered between the island and the mainland to the north-east, this vast, calm bay is studded with hundreds of dramatic limestone karsts — sheer-sided towers and jungle-cloaked islets that erupt vertically from the shallow jade-green water, riddled with caves. The most famous is Koh Tapu, the slender rock pinnacle made globally famous as 'James Bond Island' by the 1974 film The Man with the Golden Gun, and it remains the headline photo stop on any tour. But the true magic of the bay lies in its hidden 'hongs' — collapsed cave systems forming secret tidal lagoons enclosed by cliffs, accessible only by sea canoe through low, dark tunnels that open into silent, emerald worlds of mangrove and birdsong, where a guide paddles you in to marvel at the hidden inner sanctuaries. Many tours also visit Koh Panyee, a fascinating Muslim fishing village built entirely on stilts over the water, complete with its own floating football pitch, where lunch is often served. Tours range from large motor-boat trips to far more rewarding small-group and sea-kayak excursions, and the latter, which get you into the caves and lagoons, are well worth the extra cost. The scenery is at its best in the softer light and calmer conditions of early morning, before the fleets of day boats converge. Whether you canoe the caves, swim in the lagoons, photograph James Bond Island or simply gaze up at the towering karsts, Phang Nga Bay is an unforgettable, bucket-list day and arguably the most scenically stunning trip on offer.",
