    fullDescription: "The Similan Islands, a protected marine national park of eleven granite-and-jungle islands scattered in the Andaman Sea north-west of Phuket, are widely regarded as home to the most beautiful beaches and the best snorkelling and diving in all of Thailand, making a day trip here the top choice for nature lovers and underwater enthusiasts. Unlike the dramatic limestone karst scenery of Phang Nga and Phi Phi, the Similans are defined by their huge, smooth, rounded granite boulders, dazzling powder-white sand and water of extraordinary clarity — a pale, luminous turquoise over the shallow reefs that deepens to sapphire. Above the water, viewpoints such as Sail Rock on Island 8 reward a short scramble with a classic Thailand-postcard panorama over the islands and sea; below it lies a world-class underwater realm of vibrant hard and soft corals, swirling schools of colourful fish, sea turtles, reef sharks and rays that draws divers and snorkellers from across the globe. Because the park is strictly protected and exposed to rough monsoon seas, it is open only for the dry half of the year, roughly mid-October to mid-May, and closed entirely in the low season, so trips can only be taken in that window. A day trip involves an early start and a road transfer to a pier north of Phuket, followed by a fast speedboat ride out, making for a long day with plenty of travel, but for those who prioritise pristine nature, superb snorkelling and diving, and beautiful, unspoilt beaches over nightlife and shopping, the reward is well worth the effort. National-park fees apply on top of the tour price, and booking ahead is wise as the season and numbers are limited.",
