    fullDescription: "Taxis and tuk-tuks are a ubiquitous and convenient way to get around Phuket, but they come with a significant catch: they are notoriously expensive by Thai standards and almost never use meters, instead operating on negotiated flat fares that are frequently inflated for tourists, so knowing what to expect and being prepared to bargain is essential. Phuket's tuk-tuks are distinctive — unlike the open three-wheelers of Bangkok, they are typically small, brightly coloured converted vans or covered trucks — and they, along with the saloon-car taxis, are controlled by powerful and well-organised local cartels that keep prices high and have long resisted competition from cheaper metered taxis and ride-hailing apps. As a result, even short journeys can cost surprisingly large sums: a brief tuk-tuk hop within a resort area might be 200 to 400 baht, while a taxi between beaches (say Patong to Kata) can run 500 to 800 baht or more, prices that feel steep compared with the rest of Thailand and with the Grab app. The golden rules are to always agree the fare clearly before you get in — never assume there is a meter — to have a sense of the going rate for your journey, to be prepared to negotiate and to walk away if a price is outrageous, and where possible to compare against the Grab app fare as a benchmark. For late-night trips, short hops when no Grab is available, or the novelty of a tuk-tuk ride, they are useful, and drivers are generally friendly enough, but for regular getting around, using Grab, renting a scooter, or arranging transfers usually works out considerably cheaper. Carry small notes, and factor these transport costs into your budget, as they can add up quickly over a stay.",
