    fullDescription: "Songthaews — the name means 'two rows', referring to the two bench seats in the back — are Phuket's cheap and characterful local buses, converted pickup trucks or small trucks with an open-sided covered rear and bench seating, and they are the budget traveller's friend for getting around the island for a fraction of the cost of taxis and tuk-tuks. On Phuket these are often larger, bus-like blue (and other coloured) trucks that run fixed routes primarily radiating out from the central market area in Phuket Town to the main beaches — Patong, Karon, Kata, Kamala, Rawai and others — with fares of just 30 to 50 baht per journey, making them by far the cheapest way to travel between the town and the coast. To use them, you simply wait along the main route roads or at the Phuket Town terminus, flag down the one heading your way (the destination is usually signed), hop in the back, and pay the conductor or driver, often when you get off. They are a fun, authentic and economical local experience. The significant drawbacks, however, are that they are slow, stopping frequently and not always following the most direct route; they mostly run only during daytime hours, roughly from early morning until early evening, so they are no use for nights out; and crucially, most routes connect the beaches to Phuket Town rather than running directly between the beaches themselves, so a beach-to-beach journey may require going into town and back out, which is impractical. For budget-conscious travellers making daytime trips between a beach and Phuket Town, though, songthaews offer unbeatable value and a slice of local life. Just allow plenty of time, carry small change, and don't rely on them after dark.",
