    fullDescription: "As an island surrounded by some of the most beautiful seas and islands in the world, Phuket's real transport adventures happen on the water, and its piers on the east coast are the gateway to the spectacular Andaman islands and marine parks. For most visitors, the easiest and most popular way to experience the islands is via the countless organised day tours, which handle all the logistics: these packages typically include hotel pickup and drop-off, the boat trip itself (usually a fast speedboat, sometimes a larger cruiser or a traditional longtail), guides, snorkelling equipment, lunch and national-park fees, and whisk you out to destinations like the Phi Phi Islands, Phang Nga Bay and James Bond Island, the Similan Islands, the Racha Islands, Coral Island and Krabi, priced from around 1,000 to 2,500 baht per person depending on the destination and level of service. Speedboats are the fastest and most common tour vessel, reaching Phi Phi in around 50 minutes, though they can be a bumpy ride in choppier seas. For those wanting to travel independently or stay overnight on an island rather than take a day tour, scheduled passenger ferries operate on set routes, most usefully the regular ferries from Phuket's main Rassada pier (near Phuket Town) across to Phi Phi (from around 400 baht one way, taking roughly two hours), and onward to Krabi, Koh Lanta and elsewhere; these are cheaper and let you set your own itinerary but are slower and less flexible. The main departure points are Rassada, Chalong, Bang Rong and Ao Po piers, all on the calmer eastern side of the island. Do note that the seas and many boat trips, especially to exposed destinations like the Similans, are heavily affected by the monsoon season, with rougher conditions and some routes and parks closing entirely from around May to October, so island plans are best made for the calmer November-to-April dry season.",
