    fullDescription: "Phuket flight prices follow a clear seasonal pattern driven by the island's weather and the northern-hemisphere holiday calendar, and understanding it can save a good deal of money. The most expensive time to fly is the peak dry season, from around November to February, when the weather is at its glorious best — sunny, dry and warm — and demand from holidaymakers escaping the northern winter is at its highest; fares peak sharply around the Christmas and New Year period and Chinese New Year, when the island is busiest and priciest. Conversely, the cheapest fares are found during the low or 'green' season, roughly May to October, which coincides with the south-west monsoon; within this, the heart of the wet season around June, July and August typically sees the lowest prices of the year, as fewer visitors come and airlines and hotels drop rates to fill seats and rooms. The shoulder months of April, May and October offer a middle ground. It is worth remembering that the green season, while wetter, is far from a washout — rain often comes in short, heavy bursts rather than all-day downpours, the landscape is lush, the sea can still be warm, and the island is quieter and much better value, so a low-season trip can be a real bargain for those willing to accept some rain. To secure the best deal whenever you travel, book several weeks in advance for the sweet spot between price and availability, aim for midweek departures which are usually cheaper than weekends, stay flexible with your dates and use fare alerts to catch drops, and avoid the peak holiday periods if you can. Note that most long-haul visitors from Europe, the US, the UK and Australia will connect through a hub such as Bangkok, Singapore, Dubai, Doha or Hong Kong, so comparing the total connecting fare is key.",
