    fullDescription: "One of the greatest joys of Phuket is how cheaply and well you can eat, provided you follow local habits — though costs vary enormously depending on where and what you choose. At the budget end, Phuket's street food and local markets are a delicious bargain: a plate of pad thai, a bowl of noodle soup, a curry over rice, a papaya salad or a portion of the island's famous local specialities from a street stall, market vendor or humble local restaurant costs just 40 to 120 baht, and eating this way you can feast for a few dollars a day while enjoying some of the best food of your trip. Simple, authentic local restaurants (the kind with plastic chairs and a Thai clientele) charge around 100 to 250 baht for a main dish, still excellent value. Prices climb as you move upmarket and towards Western tastes: tourist-oriented restaurants in the resort strips, beachfront dining and international or Western food (pizzas, burgers, steaks) typically run 250 to 600 baht a dish, while resort restaurants, fine dining and fashionable venues charge Western-level prices of 400 to well over 1,000 baht per main, with the island's top tables far higher. The single biggest surprise for many visitors is the cost of alcohol, which is expensive relative to food due to Thai taxes: a local beer (Chang, Singha, Leo) costs 70 to 150 baht in a shop or cheap bar but more in tourist bars and clubs, a cocktail in a beach club or nightclub can be 250 to 500 baht, and imported wines and spirits are pricey, so a night of drinking can easily cost more than your meals. Coffee-shop culture is big, with a coffee at 60 to 120 baht. The clear money-saving strategy is to embrace local Thai food at local prices — it is both the cheapest and often the tastiest option — reserving Western food, resort dining and beach clubs for occasional treats, and to be mindful that alcohol is where budgets can quietly blow out.",
