    fullDescription: "Transport is one area where Phuket can cost more than visitors expect, thanks to the island's notoriously expensive and powerful taxi and tuk-tuk cartels, so it pays to understand the options and budget accordingly. At the cheap end, the local songthaews (converted-truck buses) are unbeatable value at just 30 to 50 baht a ride, but they only run daytime routes mostly connecting the beaches to Phuket Town rather than beach-to-beach, and they are slow. Renting a scooter is the cheapest way to get around freely, at 200 to 350 baht a day plus cheap petrol, but comes with serious safety risks and insurance caveats and suits only experienced riders. For most independent travellers, the ride-hailing app Grab offers the best combination of value, convenience and transparency, with fixed fares shown upfront — a short local hop might be 80 to 150 baht and longer cross-island journeys a few hundred baht — usually much cheaper than the alternatives, though availability can be patchy in some areas and at peak times. The island's local taxis and distinctive tuk-tuks are convenient and everywhere but expensive and almost never metered, running on negotiated flat fares that are often inflated for tourists: a tuk-tuk hop within a resort area might be 200 to 400 baht and a taxi between beaches 500 to 800 baht or more, so always agree the price before getting in and be ready to bargain. Hiring a car (from around 1,000–1,500 baht a day) or a private driver for the day (2,000–3,500 baht) offers comfort and flexibility, good value for families and groups. Airport transfers run from 100–200 baht per person on a shared minibus up to 600–1,300 baht for a private car or taxi. The bottom line: if you plan to move around a lot, factor transport into your budget, lean on Grab and songthaews for value, always fix taxi and tuk-tuk fares in advance, and consider basing yourself somewhere walkable to reduce the need to travel.",
