    fullDescription: "The high or dry season, running from around November to February, is unquestionably the best time to visit Phuket weather-wise, and it is when the island is at its glorious tropical best. During these months the south-west monsoon has passed, leaving the skies clear and blue, the humidity comfortably lower than at other times of year, rainfall minimal, and the sun shining reliably day after day. Daytime temperatures are warm but pleasant, typically around 31 to 33 degrees Celsius, cooling to a balmy 23 to 25 degrees at night, ideal for long days on the beach and warm evenings out. Crucially for a destination whose greatest assets are its seas and islands, this is when the Andaman Sea is at its calmest and clearest: the water is flat, safe and inviting for swimming, snorkelling and diving, visibility underwater is excellent, and the island-hopping day trips to Phi Phi, Phang Nga Bay, the Similans (which are only open in this window) and beyond run in ideal, smooth conditions, making it the prime time for the boat excursions that are central to the Phuket experience. The trade-off for this perfect weather is that it is peak tourist season: the island is at its busiest, the beaches, restaurants and attractions at their most crowded, and flight and hotel prices at their highest, spiking sharply around the Christmas, New Year and Chinese New Year holidays when Phuket is packed and expensive. To enjoy the best of the weather, book your flights, accommodation and popular tours well in advance, especially over the festive peaks, and expect to share the island's charms with plenty of other visitors. For guaranteed sunshine, calm seas and the full range of activities, the dry season is the classic and most reliable choice.",
