    fullDescription: "The period from roughly March into April, sometimes extending into early May, is Phuket's hot season — the hottest and most humid stretch of the year, forming a transitional shoulder period between the perfect dry season and the arrival of the monsoon rains. During these months the weather remains largely dry and sunny, and the sea stays generally calm and good for swimming and island trips, so it retains many of the advantages of the high season, but the heat and humidity build noticeably and can become intense, with daytime temperatures frequently climbing to 33 to 36 degrees Celsius or higher, and the increasing humidity making it feel hotter and stickier still. Towards the end of the period, the first pre-monsoon storms may begin to appear, with occasional heavy but usually short-lived downpours and the odd thundery afternoon, hinting at the wetter season to come, though these are still the exception rather than the rule. This shoulder season can be an appealing time to visit for several reasons: the sea and skies are still mostly cooperative for beach and boat activities, the fierce peak-season crowds of the Christmas and New Year period have thinned, and flight and hotel prices ease back to more moderate shoulder-season levels, offering better value than the December-to-February peak. The main consideration is simply the heat: sightseeing, walking and any strenuous activity in the middle of the day can be draining, so it pays to adopt a sensible rhythm of beach and water time, stay well hydrated, seek shade and air-conditioning during the hottest hours, and apply plenty of sun protection. A highlight of this season is the exuberant Songkran festival (Thai New Year) in mid-April, when the whole island erupts into a giant, joyful water fight — huge fun, though worth planning around as it is busy. For warm, mostly dry weather with fewer crowds and better prices, and if you can handle the heat, the hot season is a decent-value option.",
