    fullDescription: "Understanding Phuket's sea conditions is genuinely important for a safe holiday, as the island's beautiful beaches conceal a serious seasonal hazard that catches out unwary visitors every year. During the dry season, from roughly November to April, the Andaman Sea on Phuket's west coast is usually calm, flat and safe for swimming, which is one of the joys of that time of year. However, during the south-west monsoon of the green season, from around May to October, the sea changes character completely: strong winds drive in large waves and, most dangerously, create powerful rip currents — fast, narrow channels of water flowing away from the shore that can quickly drag even strong swimmers out to sea. These rip currents are the cause of numerous tourist drownings on Phuket every single year, particularly on the popular west-coast beaches such as Patong, Karon, Kata, Kamala and Surin, and they are frequently underestimated by visitors who assume a beautiful beach must be safe. To stay safe, you must understand and respect the beach flag system: a red flag means it is dangerous and you must not swim, full stop; two red flags mean the beach is closed to swimming; a yellow flag indicates caution; and green means conditions are safer, though you should still be careful. Never, ever swim past or ignore a red flag, however tempting the water looks or however confident a swimmer you are, as people die doing exactly that. Where lifeguards are present, heed their instructions and swim only in the areas they designate, between the flags. If you do get caught in a rip current, the advice is not to fight it by swimming straight back to shore, which exhausts you, but to stay calm, float, and swim parallel to the beach to escape the narrow current before heading in, or signal for help. Also take care with jet-skis and boat traffic near shore, watch children closely, and be aware that some beaches lack lifeguards entirely. Treated with respect and common sense, Phuket's seas are a delight; treated carelessly, especially in monsoon season, they can be deadly.",
