    fullDescription: "The best time to visit Phuket depends on what you value most, but for the ideal combination of weather and conditions, the clear winner is the dry, high season from November to February, and especially the December-to-February window. In these months you get near-perfect tropical weather — reliable sunshine, warm days around 31 to 33 degrees, low humidity and minimal rain — together with the calm, clear, safe seas that are essential for enjoying Phuket's greatest assets: swimming, snorkelling, diving and the island-hopping boat trips to Phi Phi, Phang Nga and the Similans (the last of which are only open in this season). The catch is that this is peak season, so you will pay the highest prices and share the island with the biggest crowds, with a particular spike over the Christmas, New Year and Chinese New Year holidays. If your priority is the best balance of value and reasonable conditions, target the shoulder periods: April and, at the other end, October and early November. March and April bring hot but mostly dry weather with calm seas and easing crowds and prices, though the heat can be intense (and mid-April brings the fun Songkran water festival). May, June and, if you don't mind more rain, the rest of the green season offer the lowest prices and quietest beaches of all, with rain often falling in short bursts rather than washing out whole days, making it excellent value for budget-conscious travellers and those who prefer a calmer island — the main caveats being rougher, sometimes unsafe seas (heed the red flags), some bumpier or cancelled boat trips, and the closure of the Similans. In short: for guaranteed sun, calm seas and full activities, book November to February and accept the crowds and cost; for the best value-to-weather balance, aim for April, May, June or October; and whenever you go, plan island trips and swimming around the season's sea conditions. Phuket is a rewarding year-round destination, but matching your timing to your priorities makes all the difference.",
